> > same description, would using such a spoiler, now accurate for all
> > games, be considered 'undue abuse'?
>
> Well said!  Following this logic, it is an abuse to remember from game
> to game that Yellow Molds cause hallucination, that kobold corpses are
> poisonous, and that eating a dog corpse causes aggravation.

If the molds that caused hallucination were randomly shuffled for
each game, that would be valid logic.  They aren't.  If the types of
corpses that are poisonous without conferring poison resistance
were randomly shuffled for each game same deal.

Stuff that doesn't vary from game to game isn't interesting to write
notes about.  So a "crude dagger" is always an "orcish dagger" and
so on.  That is admittedly the realm of the milder parts of the
spoilers.

Taking notes to defeat random shuffling isn't on the same level.
If takes some skill to be able to ID items without identify scrolls or
spell.  Taking notes on that reduces the chances that you will
acquire that skill.  Nethack is all about acquiring the types of skills
that apply across games.



